cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "layerbn: layered Bayesian networks for cohort studies"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Overmars
    given-names: Malin
    name-suffix: PhD
    affiliation: "UMC Utrecht, Vascular Cognitive Impairment research group"
repository-code: "https://github.com/umcu/layerbn"
license: MIT
version: 3.0.0
date-released: "2026-11-27"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.17302710
    description: "Concept DOI for all versions"
abstract: >-
  layerbn learns Bayesian networks from cohort data under a structure
  specified by the investigator. Variables are grouped into ordered layers,
  and the learner may only draw an arc from a layer to itself or to a layer
  further down that order, so the recovered structure respects the assumed
  order of influence. Variables measured more than once can optionally be
  listed once with their time points, and the layers are generated in time
  order.
  Beyond the layer order, individual arcs may be forbidden or required, and
  variables may be pinned as roots or sinks; such rules can only narrow what
  the order permits. The analysis is described in a variables table and a
  settings file instead of code, so that it can be published with a
  manuscript. The package provides bootstrap estimates of arc stability,
  mutual-information rankings of variables against each target, outcome
  probabilities for defined profiles with bootstrap intervals, and
  single-variable sensitivity analysis. A command line produces figures,
  tables and a report. The method is general and applies to any study whose
  variables can be arranged in ordered layers. It was first applied to a
  vascular cognitive impairment cohort, and was released under the name
  vcibayes up to version 1.2.1.
keywords:
  - Bayesian network
  - structure learning
  - cohort study
  - epidemiology
  - pyAgrum
